 <h3 class=3D"newsTitle grey_blue" style=3D"text-transform: uppercase;">The=
 Zorro Project: September 2026</h3></div>
 <p>John Ehlers is well known for his no-lag indicators, and this one=E2=80=
=94introduced in his article in this issue, =E2=80=9CAdaptive SuperSmoother=
=E2=80=9D=E2=80=94is his latest invention. With this new indicator version =
of his, the time period of Ehlers=E2=80=99 standard SuperSmoother indicator=
 is adapted to the <em>average rate of price change</em>.</p>=20
<p>Ehlers=E2=80=99 standard SuperSmoother indicator already exists in the Z=
orro indicator library. Here, I am providing coding for use in the Zorro pl=
atform for his new adaptive version of the indicator. The code is a 1:1 tra=
nslation of the EasyLanguage code given in Ehlers=E2=80=99 article to C:</p=
>
<pre>var SmoothA(vars Data, int Period0)
{
	static var Period =3D 20;
	var SS =3D Smooth(Data,Period);
	var ROC1 =3D SS-ref(SS,1);
	var ROCRMS =3D sqrt(SumSq(series(ROC1),81)/81);
	var ROC2 =3D abs(ROC1/fix0(ROCRMS));
	ROC2 =3D min(ROC2,2);
	Period =3D Period0*(1.-.5*ROC2)*(1.-.5*ROC2);
	Period =3D max(Period,2);
	return SS;
}
</pre>
<p>The <em>period</em> variable is declared static because it needs to keep=
 its value between function calls. We use the following code to apply the a=
daptive SuperSmoother (blue), and a nonadapive one (red), and the differenc=
e to an S&amp;P 500 chart from 2024, which is pictured in Figure 7.</p>
<div class=3D"figBorder">
<img src=3D"https://traders.com/Documentation/FEEDbk_docs/2026/09/images/TT=
-Zorro.gif" alt=3D"Sample Chart" class=3D"shadow1" style=3D"width: 610px;">=
<p class=3D"figCaption" style=3D"width: 550px;">FIGURE 7: ZORRO. This shows=
 an example S&amp;P 500 chart plotting the adaptive SuperSmoother (blue), a=
 nonadaptive SuperSmoother (red), and the difference to form an oscillator =
(lower subgraph).</p></div>
<pre>void run()=20
{
	BarPeriod =3D 1440;=20
	LookBack =3D 100;
	StartDate =3D 20240901;=20
	EndDate =3D 20250901;
	asset("SPX500");
	var Smooth1 =3D SmoothA(seriesC(),20);
	var Smooth2 =3D Smooth(seriesC(),20);
	plot("SmoothA",Smooth1,LINE,BLUE);
	plot("Smooth",Smooth2,LINE,RED);
	plot("Diff",Smooth1-Smooth2,NEW|LINE,RED);
}
</pre>
<p>The resulting chart, as shown in Figure 7, replicates Ehlers=E2=80=99 ch=
art in his article. We can see that the blue line tightly follows the price=
 curve.</p>
